locale = "en"

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.a1"
text = "No. They are made of quarks. A proton is two u quarks and one d quark (uud); a neutron is one u and two d (udd). The u quark has charge \\(\\ +\\frac{{2}}{{3}}e\\) and the d quark \\(\\ -\\frac{{1}}{{3}}e\\), so a proton totals \\(\\ +e\\) and a neutron totals 0. Quarks are never observed alone (confinement), so the smallest free charge in nature is still \\(\\ e\\). As far as we know, the electron really is elementary."

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.a2"
text = "Not in ordinary conditions. Protons sit inside nuclei, and changing them takes nuclear reactions. Charging always means moving electrons, so a positively charged body is one that is short of electrons, not one that has extra protons."

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.answer"
text = "Opposite charges move toward each other, and they speed up all the way to the collision: the closer they are, the stronger the force. Like charges fly apart, and their acceleration fades because the force falls with distance. Try it: left click and right click on empty space, then L unpins a charge if it is pinned."

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 1"

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.legend1"
text = "the charge of a body — a signed number. In SI it is measured in coulombs (C)."

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.legend2"
text = "the elementary charge, the smallest free portion of charge in nature."

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.legend3"
text = "a whole number of portions: 0, 1, 2, …"

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.legend4"
text = "the total charge of an isolated system. It does not change with time."

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.legend5"
text = "like signs — repulsion."

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.legend6"
text = "opposite signs — attraction."

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.note"
text = "The elementary charge is tiny: one coulomb is about 6.24 billion billion elementary charges."

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.p1"
text = "Electric charge is the quantity that says how a body takes part in electric interaction. Charge has a sign: positive or negative. Like signs repel; opposite signs attract."

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.p2"
text = "Charge is quantized: any free charge is a whole number of elementary portions \\(\\ e\\) — the charge of a proton (\\(+e\\)) or an electron (\\(-e\\)). Ordinary matter is neutral because it holds equal numbers of protons and electrons. Charging a body means moving some electrons, by friction or by contact. A field near a conductor only redistributes the electrons inside it; to change the body's total charge, the body also has to be grounded."

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.p3"
text = "Charge is conserved. In an isolated system the sum of all charges stays constant. Charges can pass from one body to another, but they do not appear or vanish one at a time."

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.p4"
text = "Orange “+” circles are positive charges, blue “−” circles are negative. The radius grows with \\(\\ |q|\\). Left click places \\(\\ +q\\), right click places \\(\\ -q\\) (the amount is the “\\(|q|\\) of new charges” slider), the wheel over a charge changes \\(\\ |q|\\), and F flips the sign."

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.p5"
text = "Charges in the program are in arbitrary units, not coulombs. That is only a choice of scale: every relation among forces, fields, and energies is the same as in SI (see the Units section)."

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.predict"
text = "Place two free charges, \\(\\ +1\\) and \\(\\ -1\\), a few cells apart and run the simulation. What happens? What if both are \\(\\ +1\\)?"

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.q1"
text = "Are the proton and the neutron elementary particles?"

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.q2"
text = "Can you charge a body by changing how many protons it has?"

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.title"
text = "Electric charge"

[[string]]
id = "help.charge.try"
text = "Try it: “Swarm” — opposite charges gather into clusters"

[[string]]
id = "help.close"
text = "Close"

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.a1"
text = "“The energy of a charge”, \\(\\ q\\varphi\\), is the energy of its interaction with the others when those others are treated as fixed. Configuration energy is the energy of the whole system, in which any charge may move. If both charges are free, the energy that is released is shared between them (so that momentum is conserved), not handed to only one. So \\(\\ q\\varphi\\) is a useful approximation, and the energy belongs to the pair, or to the system."

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.a2"
text = "Because the same charge has a different energy in a different arrangement. The energy depends on the distances to the others, not on the charge alone. There is no place “inside the charge” where it would sit. If one still asks “where”, the answer is in the field between the charges — and that is not a substance, but a state of space."

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.a3"
text = "The energy of the field is the configuration energy, stored once when the configuration was assembled. While the charges stay still, it goes nowhere and nothing is spent. It changes only when the configuration changes (the charges move) or when the field leaves as a wave — radiation (topic 3)."

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.answer1"
text = "Three pairs, each with energy \\(\\ k\\frac{{1\\cdot 1}}{{1}} = 1\\), so \\(\\ U = 3\\). That is the work of bringing the charges in from infinity, and the kinetic energy they gain if they fly apart."

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.answer2"
text = "\\(-0.5\\) at distance 2, and \\(\\ -0.25\\) at distance 4. The magnitude is half as large when the distance doubles, and the sign is negative because the charges are opposite."

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 14"

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.legend1"
text = "the potential at charge i due to every other charge (not its own)."

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.legend2"
text = "each pair is counted twice in the sum over i — hence the factor of one half."

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.legend3"
text = "the energy density of the field: energy per unit volume of space."

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.legend4"
text = "the sum of that density over all space."

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.note"
text = "A subtle point: adding up u over all space around a point charge gives infinity, because the field grows without bound at the point. This “self-energy” is a known trouble in classical theory; quantum electrodynamics works around it. In the program, charges are balls of finite size (softening), so there are no infinities. The interaction energy on the panel does not include self-energy."

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.p1"
text = "Potential energy belongs to the system, not to one charge — to how all the charges are arranged. \\(\\ U = q\\varphi\\) for a single charge is a convenient shorthand when the sources are fixed (a pinned nucleus, for example): only the position of \\(\\ q\\) changes, so the whole change can be “assigned” to it. What it really is, though, is the energy of \\(\\ q\\) interacting with those sources."

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.p2"
text = "For many charges the energy is a sum over every pair. The same sum can be written as the sum of \\(\\ q\\varphi\\) for each charge in the potential of the rest, divided by two: in that sum every pair is counted twice."

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.p3"
text = "Where does this energy sit? The modern answer is: in the field itself. Every bit of space that holds a field \\(\\ E\\) carries energy with a density proportional to the square of the field (the formula below). For a smooth charge distribution the total over all space equals the interaction energy. For point charges that integral diverges because of each charge's self-energy (the note below); the panel shows only the finite energy of the pairs. The clearest evidence that a field really carries energy is sunlight: an electromagnetic field crossing empty space and delivering energy to the Earth, with no charges along the way."

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.p4"
text = "“Potential \\(\\ U\\)” on the panel is the energy of the whole configuration, the sum over pairs. It changes whenever any charge moves — whether the field moves it, or you drag it with the mouse."

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.predict1"
text = "How much energy does it take to assemble three charges of \\(\\ +1\\) at the corners of an equilateral triangle of side 1 (\\(k = 1\\))?"

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.predict2"
text = "A pair \\(\\ +1\\) and \\(\\ +1\\) at distance 2 has \\(\\ U = 0.5\\). What is the energy of \\(\\ +1\\) and \\(\\ -1\\) at the same distance? And at distance 4?"

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.q1"
text = "How is the energy of one charge different from the energy of a configuration?"

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.q2"
text = "Why is it a bad picture to think of potential energy as stuff stored inside a charge?"

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.q3"
text = "How does this relate to whether a field spends energy just by existing?"

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.title"
text = "Configuration energy"

[[string]]
id = "help.config_energy.try"
text = "Try it: “Swarm” — configuration energy turns into motion"

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.a1"
text = "No. The scene is a classical model: it is how two charged balls would move in a vacuum. An electron in an atom follows quantum mechanics and has no trajectory. Its state is a wave function \\(\\ \\psi\\), and \\(\\ |\\psi|^2\\) is the probability density of finding the electron at a place. An orbital is a region where that probability is large. A classical charge on a circular orbit is also accelerating, so it would radiate electromagnetic waves: such a “classical atom” would lose its energy and fall into the nucleus in about a hundred-billionth of a second. The stability of atoms is one reason quantum mechanics was needed."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.a2"
text = "In a model of point charges, they do: as \\(\\ r \\to 0\\) the force grows without bound. That is why the simulation has softening and collisions (see the appendix). In reality an electron does not fall into a proton, because of quantum effects. Squeezing an electron into a smaller region takes more kinetic energy (the uncertainty relation), and the balance of that energy against Coulomb attraction sets the size of an atom. The fields are not what holds them apart: each charge’s field fills all of space, and fields of different charges always overlap and simply add, at any distance. The exception is a particle and its antiparticle: an electron and a positron can meet and annihilate into photons."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.answer1"
text = "The attraction doubles, while the speed at that instant stays the same. The force is then stronger than a circle of that radius needs, so the charge starts to fall inward. The orbit becomes elongated (an ellipse), and the point where you changed \\(\\ k\\) is the farthest point from the center."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.answer2"
text = "Triple the distance and the force is 9 times smaller. Double one charge and the force is twice as large. Together: 2/9 of the original, about 4.5 times weaker."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 2"

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.legend1"
text = "the magnitude of the interaction force."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.legend2"
text = "Coulomb’s constant. In the program \\(\\ k = 1\\) (the slider changes it). The SI value is below."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.legend3"
text = "the charges, with their signs. The sign of the product sets the direction: plus is repulsion, minus is attraction."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.legend4"
text = "the distance between the charges."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.legend5"
text = "the vector from charge 2 to charge 1. Dividing by \\(\\ r\\) turns it into a unit direction."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.legend6"
text = "the force with which charge 2 acts on charge 1."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.note1"
text = "Coulomb’s constant in SI. The factor \\(\\ 4\\pi\\) is the area of a unit sphere; the denominator also holds the electric constant."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.note2"
text = "The same shape as the law of gravity, except that mass has one sign and charge has two. The electric interaction is also vastly stronger: between a proton and an electron it exceeds gravity by about 39 orders of magnitude."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.note3"
text = "An example in the program: \\(\\ k = 1\\), both charges equal to 1, distance 2. Doubling the distance cuts the force by four."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.p1"
text = "Two charges at rest push or pull each other with a force proportional to the product of the charges and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them. The force lies along the line that joins them: away from the other charge when the signs match, toward it when they differ."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.p2"
text = "The square of the distance comes from geometry. The influence of a point charge spreads equally in every direction, over a sphere of radius \\(\\ r\\). The area of a sphere grows as the square of the radius, so the share per unit area falls as the inverse square of the distance. That is also where the \\(\\ 4\\pi\\) in Coulomb’s constant comes from. The same geometry gives the same dependence for gravity and for illumination."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.p3"
text = "The two forces are mutual and opposite (Newton’s third law). Forces from several charges add as vectors — that is superposition (topic 4)."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.p4"
text = "On every simulation step the program computes this force for every pair of charges and adds up the forces on each charge. The slider “\\(k\\) — Coulomb constant” scales every force at once."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.p5"
text = "In the “Orbit” scene the Coulomb force plays the role of the centripetal force: it always points at the center and holds the negative charge on a circular orbit around the positive one."

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.predict1"
text = "In the “Orbit” scene the charge moves in a circle. What happens if you double \\(\\ k\\) with the slider while it is moving?"

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.predict2"
text = "How does the force between two charges change if you triple the distance and double one of the charges?"

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.q1"
text = "Does an electron really orbit a nucleus the way the charge does in the “Orbit” scene?"

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.q2"
text = "Why don’t opposite charges fall into each other?"

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.title"
text = "Coulomb’s law"

[[string]]
id = "help.coulomb.try"
text = "Try it: “Orbit” — Coulomb attraction as a centripetal force"

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.a1"
text = "The same way a Celsius temperature or a height below sea level can be negative: the number is negative only relative to a chosen zero. A negative \\(\\ U\\) for a pair of opposite charges means their energy is lower than the energy of those same charges pulled infinitely far apart. There is no “negative substance of energy” behind it."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.a2"
text = "Because physics only sees changes in \\(\\ U\\). Force is the slope of \\(\\ U\\) (\\(F = -\\frac{{dU}}{{dx}}\\)), and the constant \\(\\ C\\) drops out. Work, the exchange with kinetic energy, and the motion all depend on \\(\\ \\Delta U\\), and a constant cancels in a difference."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.a3"
text = "Because only they can be measured. Every experiment compares two states: speed before and after, position before and after. The energy a system can give away, or the energy you must give it, is always the difference between a starting state and an ending state."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.a4"
text = "For convenience and for a single answer: each configuration then has one number, and \\(\\ U = k\\frac{{Qq}}{{r}}\\) needs no extra constant. The sign of \\(\\ U\\) then also tells you at once whether the system is bound (\\(E < 0\\))."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.answer1"
text = "Negative (about \\(\\ -0.17\\)). The charge cannot escape the central one, because that would take \\(\\ E \\geq 0\\). That is why it keeps circling — it is a bound state."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.answer2"
text = "No. Forces depend only on how \\(\\ U\\) changes with distance, and a constant drops out of that. Only the numbers on the panel would change. The trajectories would not."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 12"

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.legend1"
text = "any constant: a shift of the zero level."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.legend2"
text = "the energy difference of two states. It does not depend on the choice of zero."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.legend3"
text = "the total mechanical energy \\(\\ K + U\\), not the field strength. With the zero at infinity, \\(\\ E < 0\\) means a bound state: the charge cannot leave its partner."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.note"
text = "On a circular orbit the kinetic energy is exactly half the magnitude of the potential energy, so the total energy is negative and equal to half of \\(\\ U\\). In the “Orbit” scene, \\(\\ K \\approx 0.17,\\ U \\approx -0.33,\\ E \\approx -0.17\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.p1"
text = "The zero of potential energy is a choice, not a fact of nature. Add the same constant \\(\\ C\\) to every value of \\(\\ U\\) and nothing you can observe changes: forces, motion, and every measurement depend only on how \\(\\ U\\) changes, that is, on differences \\(\\ \\Delta U\\). Height can be measured from the floor, from a table, or from sea level — a body falls the same way."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.p2"
text = "A negative potential energy is therefore nothing strange. It only means the state lies below the zero you chose. For charges it is convenient to put the zero at infinity: the interaction vanishes there, and \\(\\ U = k\\frac{{Qq}}{{r}}\\) needs no extra constant. Negative \\(\\ U\\) then means the system has to be “lifted”, by adding energy, before the charges can be pulled apart."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.p3"
text = "That is the idea of a bound state. Here and in the later energy topics, the letter \\(\\ E\\) means the total mechanical energy \\(\\ K + U\\), not the field strength. If this \\(\\ E\\) is negative, there is not enough kinetic energy to reach infinity, where \\(\\ U = 0\\), and \\(\\ K\\) cannot be negative. The charge is stuck moving near its partner — like a planet near the Sun, or (in the classical picture) an electron near a nucleus. If \\(\\ E \\geq 0\\), it can leave forever."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.p4"
text = "The energy panel computes \\(\\ U\\) with the zero at infinity, so the sign of the total energy \\(\\ E = K + U\\) shows at once whether the system is bound. “Orbit” is a bound state with \\(\\ E < 0\\). In “Rutherford” the projectiles have \\(\\ E > 0\\) and fly off forever."

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.predict1"
text = "In the “Orbit” scene, look at the panel. What is the sign of the total energy \\(\\ E = K + U\\), and what does it mean?"

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.predict2"
text = "If you added 100 to every energy in the simulation, would the motion change?"

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.q1"
text = "How can potential energy be negative?"

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.q2"
text = "Why can the zero of potential energy be chosen freely?"

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.q3"
text = "Why, then, are the differences \\(\\ \\Delta U\\) the ones that matter physically?"

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.q4"
text = "Why agree on a zero at infinity at all?"

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.title"
text = "The zero of energy, and negative \\(\\ U\\)"

[[string]]
id = "help.energy_zero.try"
text = "Try it: “Orbit” with the potential background and trails"

[[string]]
id = "help.field.a1"
text = "In electrostatics you can read the field as a precise account of the force that would appear at each point if a charge were placed there. But a field is more than tidy bookkeeping: it carries energy, and when charges move, the change in the field spreads through space at the speed of light, not instantly. That is why the field is treated as a physical object, not only as a formula."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.a2"
text = "No. The static field of charges at rest spends nothing — just as a raised body spends nothing by staying up. Energy is spent once, to build the configuration (to pull opposite charges apart, or to push like charges together). After that it is stored in the field. Spending appears only when something moves: a current in a resistor, or radiation."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.a3"
text = "Because the sources (\\(Q\\)) create the field, and the test charge is there only to “measure” it. Dividing the force by \\(\\ q\\) removes the properties of the measuring charge from the answer. The test charge is taken to be small so that it does not shift the sources and distort the field it is measuring."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.a4"
text = "The force is proportional to the test charge: twice the \\(\\ q\\) means twice the \\(\\ F\\). The ratio \\(\\ \\frac{{F}}{{q}}\\) stays the same. The force changes. The field does not."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.a5"
text = "The field \\(\\ E\\) is a property of a point in space (the sources set it), the same for any charge that arrives there. The force \\(\\ F\\) is a property of one particular charge interacting with that field. At one point, charges \\(\\ +1\\), \\(\\ +2\\), and \\(\\ -1\\) feel forces \\(\\ E\\), \\(\\ 2E\\), and \\(\\ -E\\): one field, three forces."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.answer1"
text = "For equal charges, exactly in the middle: the fields match in size and oppose in direction. Between \\(\\ +1\\) and \\(\\ -1\\) there is no such point on the segment that joins them: the field away from the plus and the field toward the minus point the same way and add. Check it with the probe (F5) in “Like charges” and “Dipole”."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.answer2"
text = "The field does not change at all — the sources set it. Only the force changes: \\(\\ F = qE\\), so for \\(\\ q = -1\\) it points opposite the arrow \\(\\ E\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 3"

[[string]]
id = "help.field.legend1"
text = "the field strength — a vector at a point. \\(\\ |E|\\) is the force per unit charge \\(\\ (\\mathrm{{N}}/\\mathrm{{C}})\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.legend2"
text = "the source charge that creates the field."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.legend3"
text = "the test charge the field acts on. Do not confuse it with \\(\\ Q\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.legend4"
text = "the distance from the source \\(\\ Q\\) to the point."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.legend5"
text = "the force on the charge \\(\\ q\\) at this point."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.note"
text = "The second formula describes only the field of one point charge. The first and the third — the definition of the field, and the force in it — hold for any electrostatic field."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.p1"
text = "Instead of describing the force between every pair of charges, one says: source charges create an electric field in space, and the field acts on any other charge that happens to be at a point. The field is defined at every point — including places where no other charge sits."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.p2"
text = "The field strength \\(\\ E\\) is the force that would act on a test charge at that point, divided by the size of that charge. The force is proportional to the test charge, so the ratio \\(\\ \\frac{{F}}{{q}}\\) does not depend on it. It describes only the sources and the location of the point."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.p3"
text = "Once you know the field, the force on a particular charge is the product \\(\\ F = qE\\). For a positive \\(\\ q\\) the force points along \\(\\ E\\); for a negative \\(\\ q\\) it points against \\(\\ E\\). The field of a point charge points away from it when \\(\\ Q > 0\\), and toward it when \\(\\ Q < 0\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.p4"
text = "F3 turns on the vector grid: arrows of \\(\\ E\\) at the grid points. Length and color are \\(\\ |E|\\) on a logarithmic scale (yellow is a strong field, purple a weak one)."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.p5"
text = "F5 is a test charge of \\(\\ +1\\) under the cursor. A white arrow shows the direction of \\(\\ E\\), and the readout beside it shows \\(\\ |E|\\), the components of \\(\\ E\\), and the potential \\(\\ \\varphi\\). For \\(\\ q = +1\\) the numbers for \\(\\ E\\) and \\(\\ F\\) match, so the readout writes \\(\\ |E| = F/q\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.field.predict1"
text = "Between two equal charges of \\(\\ +1\\) there is a point where the field is zero. Where is it? Is there such a point between \\(\\ +1\\) and \\(\\ -1\\)?"

[[string]]
id = "help.field.predict2"
text = "A test charge of \\(\\ +1\\) shows an arrow \\(\\ E\\) at a point. What changes if you imagine a charge of \\(\\ -1\\) there instead?"

[[string]]
id = "help.field.q1"
text = "What does a field in empty space mean, physically?"

[[string]]
id = "help.field.q2"
text = "Does a field have to be fed energy all the time just to exist?"

[[string]]
id = "help.field.q3"
text = "Why is the \\(\\ q\\) in \\(\\ E = \\frac{{F}}{{q}}\\) a test charge, not the source?"

[[string]]
id = "help.field.q4"
text = "Why doesn’t changing the test charge change the field?"

[[string]]
id = "help.field.q5"
text = "How is a field different from a force?"

[[string]]
id = "help.field.title"
text = "The electric field"

[[string]]
id = "help.field.try"
text = "Try it: a dipole with the vector grid and a test charge"

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.a1"
text = "Not in general. The field sets the acceleration, not the velocity. On a straight line the charge does follow it, but on a curve inertia carries it outside the bend, and the trajectory leaves the line. Lines and trajectories match only when the speed stays very small, for example in a very viscous medium."

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.a2"
text = "At a crossing the field would have two directions at once, and at each point it has only one. The only exception is a point where \\(\\ E = 0\\) (halfway between two equal charges, for example): the direction is undefined there, and lines may meet."

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.a3"
text = "No. It is only a picture. Each flow particle’s velocity is set, at every instant, along the field, with no mass and no inertia, so they follow the lines exactly. Real charges in the simulation move by Newton’s law — with acceleration — and their trajectories show up in the trails (F6)."

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.answer"
text = "Only at the very start, while the speed is small. After that, inertia pulls it off the curved line. Check it: place a free charge (L unpins one), turn on trails (F6) and lines (F2), and compare the trail with the line."

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 5"

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.legend1"
text = "a point on the line. s is the distance traveled along the line."

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.legend2"
text = "the unit direction of the line at the point. It matches the direction of the field."

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.legend3"
text = "the number of lines leaving a charge (or entering it)."

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.p1"
text = "A field line is a curve whose tangent at every point lies along the field \\(\\ E\\). It is a way to see a vector field as a whole: the grid arrows show the field at separate points, and the lines join those directions into a continuous picture."

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.p2"
text = "Lines of an electrostatic field start on positive charges and end on negative ones (or run off to infinity). They never cross, because at each point the field has one direction. In space they are denser where the field is stronger. The number of lines leaving a charge is proportional to its size. On the screen this is only qualitative: the drawn lines thin out with distance more slowly than the field weakens (topic 6)."

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.p3"
text = "How many lines you draw per unit charge is a convention; you may choose it freely. Only the proportion has physical meaning: twice the charge, twice the lines. Gauss’s law (topic 6) is why that rule works."

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.p4"
text = "F2 turns on field lines. The program launches them from positive charges — 8 lines per unit charge — and steps them along \\(\\ E\\) with a fourth-order Runge–Kutta method, taking shorter steps near charges. A line stops on a negative charge or past the edge of the screen. Lines are also traced backward from negative charges, so the ones that arrive from infinity are shown too. The brightness of a line is the field strength; the arrows are the direction."

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.p5"
text = "F4 is an animated flow of particles along the lines. About a third are born near positive charges, and the rest appear anywhere in view. From there they all follow the field, toward a negative charge or off the screen, moving faster where the field is stronger."

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.predict"
text = "Release a free light charge in a dipole field, off to the side of the axis. Does it ride along a field line?"

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.q1"
text = "Does a charge you release travel along a field line?"

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.q2"
text = "Why don’t field lines cross?"

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.q3"
text = "The flow particles (F4) run along the lines. Is that charges moving?"

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.title"
text = "Field lines"

[[string]]
id = "help.field_lines.try"
text = "Try it: a dipole with field lines and flow"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.a1"
text = "Because flux is counted with a sign. Lines from an outside charge enter on one side and the same number leave on the other: the “in” and “out” contributions cancel. Only a charge on which lines begin or end inside the surface gives a nonzero flux."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.a2"
text = "For charges at rest the two are equivalent: each can be derived from the other. But Gauss’s law is one of Maxwell’s equations and always holds, including for moving charges, where Coulomb’s law is no longer exact. For symmetric systems (a sphere, a line, a plane) it also gives the field in one line of algebra."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.a3"
text = "Only qualitatively. Real charges are three-dimensional, and their lines spread in all three dimensions, while the program draws only the ones that lie in the plane of the screen. On the screen the lines thin out with distance more slowly than the field weakens. The vector grid (F3) and the probe (F5) show the true magnitude."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.answer1"
text = "Between the rows of charges the field is strong and nearly uniform: the lines run parallel from the pluses to the minuses. Outside, the fields of the two rows nearly cancel, so the field is weak. Near the edges the lines bend outward — the edge effects of finite plates. Check it with the probe (F5): along the middle, \\(\\ |E|\\) hardly changes."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.answer2"
text = "Zero: the total charge inside is \\(\\ +1 - 1 = 0\\). Every line that leaves the plus through the sphere comes back through the same sphere to the minus."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 6"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.legend1"
text = "the flux of the field through a closed surface."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.legend2"
text = "the sum (the integral) over the whole closed surface."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.legend3"
text = "a small patch of the surface: a vector pointing outward, with length equal to its area."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.legend4"
text = "the total charge inside the surface. Charges outside are not included."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.legend5"
text = "the electric constant; \\(\\ k = \\frac{{1}}{{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}}\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.legend6"
text = "the surface charge density — charge per unit area of a plate."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.note"
text = "The last formula is the field between two large plates with opposite charge (a capacitor). It is the same at every point between them and nearly zero outside — the uniform field of topic 9."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.p1"
text = "The flux of a field through a surface says how much field “passes through” it. In the picture of field lines, it is how many lines cross the surface, with a sign (outward is plus, inward is minus). More precisely, it is the sum of the field’s normal component times the area, over every small patch of the surface."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.p2"
text = "Gauss’s law: the flux through any closed surface equals the charge inside it, divided by \\(\\ \\varepsilon_0\\). The shape of the surface does not matter, and neither do charges outside: however many lines from an outside charge enter the surface, the same number leave. That is why the number of lines leaving a charge is proportional to its size."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.p3"
text = "Coulomb’s law follows from Gauss’s law. Surround a point charge with a sphere. By symmetry the field on the sphere is uniform and radial, so the flux equals the field times the area of the sphere. The field therefore falls as the inverse square of the distance — the lines do not disappear, they only spread over a larger and larger sphere."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.p4"
text = "The rule “8 field lines per unit charge” (F2) is Gauss’s law applied directly: the number of lines from each charge is proportional to the charge. The “Capacitor” scene is two rows of opposite charges, an approximation to two plates with a uniform field between them."

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.predict1"
text = "In the “Capacitor” scene, where is the field strongest and most uniform? What happens outside, and near the edges?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.predict2"
text = "What is the flux through a sphere that encloses a whole dipole?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.q1"
text = "If a charge sits outside the surface, the field on the surface is not zero. Why is the flux zero?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.q2"
text = "Why bother with Gauss’s law if Coulomb’s law exists?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.q3"
text = "Can you read the field strength off the density of lines in the program?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.title"
text = "Flux and Gauss’s law"

[[string]]
id = "help.gauss.try"
text = "Try it: “Capacitor” with field lines and flow"

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.a1"
text = "The field is the spatial rate of change of the potential, with a minus sign. The potential is a scalar “height map”; the field is its slope at each point. Each determines the other: \\(\\ E\\) is the gradient of \\(\\ \\varphi\\), and \\(\\ \\varphi\\) is the sum of \\(\\ E\\) along a path (up to a constant)."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.a2"
text = "The gradient points toward growing \\(\\ \\varphi\\), but the field pushes a positive charge toward where its energy \\(\\ U = q\\varphi\\) is smaller, that is, toward falling \\(\\ \\varphi\\). The minus turns the gradient that way. “The field points toward lower potential” is that same minus, said in words."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.a3"
text = "Yes. The field depends on how \\(\\ \\varphi\\) changes, not on its value. Halfway between two equal charges, \\(\\ \\varphi > 0\\), but that point is a saddle, so the slope is zero and \\(\\ E = 0\\). In the middle of a dipole, \\(\\ \\varphi = 0\\), yet the field there is not zero: it points from the plus to the minus. Along the line between the charges that midpoint is actually the least steep place; the slope is steepest near the charges themselves."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.answer1"
text = "Densest near the charges and between them. That is where the arrows are longest and brightest, and everywhere they cross the equipotentials at a right angle. Far from the charges the lines spread out — the field weakens."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.answer2"
text = "About \\(\\ |E| \\approx \\frac{{0.8 - 0.6}}{{0.1}} = 2\\), pointing from A to B — toward the lower potential."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 10"

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.legend1"
text = "the gradient of the potential — a vector toward the fastest growth of \\(\\ \\varphi\\), with length equal to that rate."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.legend2"
text = "how fast \\(\\ \\varphi\\) changes if you move only along \\(\\ x\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.legend3"
text = "the same link in one dimension: the field is the rate at which the potential falls."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.legend4"
text = "the same link in mechanics: force is the slope of potential energy."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.note"
text = "A rough estimate: if neighboring equipotentials differ by \\(\\ \\Delta\\varphi\\) and sit a distance \\(\\ \\Delta s\\) apart, the field between them is about this."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.p1"
text = "Potential and field are not two independent quantities. They are two descriptions of the same thing. Picture \\(\\ \\varphi\\) as a height map: every point has a number. Then the field \\(\\ E\\) is the slope of that map. At each point it says which way the potential falls fastest, and how steeply."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.p2"
text = "Mathematically that slope is the gradient: the vector \\(\\ \\nabla\\varphi\\), built from how fast \\(\\ \\varphi\\) changes along \\(\\ x\\) and along \\(\\ y\\). The gradient points where \\(\\ \\varphi\\) grows fastest, and the field points the other way, which is why the formula carries a minus. It also follows that the field is perpendicular to the equipotentials: along an equipotential, \\(\\ \\varphi\\) does not change at all."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.p3"
text = "The magnitude of the field is the steepness of the slope. The closer the equipotentials lie, the faster \\(\\ \\varphi\\) changes and the stronger the field. Where \\(\\ \\varphi\\) has a peak, a hollow, or a saddle, the slope is zero and \\(\\ E = 0\\). The link runs the other way too: given the field, the potential is the sum of \\(\\ E\\) along a path — up to a constant, that is, up to the choice of zero."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.p4"
text = "The F1 background is that same “height map”: color is \\(\\ \\varphi\\), and the thin lines are equipotentials every \\(\\ 0.25\\). The F3 vector grid shows its slope. Set them side by side and the arrows always cross the equipotentials at a right angle, and grow longer where the lines crowd together. In the program the relation is exact: potential and field are computed from one formula, and the field is precisely minus the gradient of the potential."

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.predict1"
text = "Turn on the potential background (F1) and the vector grid (F3) on a dipole. Where are the equipotentials densest, and what are the arrows doing there?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.predict2"
text = "The probe reads \\(\\ \\varphi = 0.8\\) at point A and \\(\\ \\varphi = 0.6\\) at point B, a distance 0.1 from A along the field. What is the field between them?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.q1"
text = "How exactly are the field and the potential related?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.q2"
text = "Why is there a minus in \\(\\ E = -\\nabla\\varphi\\)?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.q3"
text = "Can the field be zero where the potential is not, and the other way around?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.title"
text = "The field as the slope of the potential"

[[string]]
id = "help.gradient.try"
text = "Try it: a dipole with the potential background and the vector grid"

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.a1"
text = "Because it does not cancel. Charges of both signs neutralize each other, so a large body is almost uncharged as a whole and barely acts on others electrically. Masses only add: a planet has an enormous number of them, and they all pull the same way."

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.a2"
text = "No. An electric field can be weakened by placing opposite charges nearby (that is how a conducting shell works). Shielding gravity would take negative mass, and none has been found in nature."

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.a3"
text = "Yes, when the force attracts. Any central force that falls as \\(\\ \\sim \\frac{{1}}{{r^2}}\\) gives ellipses, parabolas, or hyperbolas, and the same Kepler laws. That is why “Orbit” looks like a planetary orbit. “Rutherford” is the same \\(\\ \\sim \\frac{{1}}{{r^2}}\\) law with repulsion: a projectile bends around the nucleus from the outside, while a comet bends around the Sun from the inside."

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.answer"
text = "At the point closest to the center (as a planet does at perihelion): \\(\\ U\\) is smallest there, so \\(\\ K\\) is largest. That is Kepler’s second law — in equal times the radius sweeps out equal areas."

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 15"

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.legend1"
text = "the gravitational constant. The energy formula carries a minus because masses only attract."

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.legend2"
text = "the masses — gravitational “charges”, always positive."

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.legend3"
text = "the gravitational field strength — the acceleration of free fall, the same for every body."

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.legend4"
text = "the charge-to-mass ratio: it sets how strongly an electric field speeds a body up."

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.p1"
text = "Newton’s law of gravity has the same shape as Coulomb’s law: the force is proportional to the product of the “charges” (for gravity, the masses) and inversely proportional to the square of the distance. So the whole construction of this reference — field as force per unit, potential as energy per unit, \\(\\ \\Delta U = -W\\) — works for gravity almost word for word."

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.p2"
text = "The main difference is the signs. Mass is only positive, and gravity only attracts. Gravitational potential energy is therefore always negative, and systems under gravity readily form bound states (planets, stars, galaxies). Electric charges come in two signs, and matter as a whole is neutral: the contributions cancel."

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.p3"
text = "Hence a paradox: the electric interaction between protons is stronger than gravity by about 36 orders of magnitude, yet on a large scale gravity rules. Charges cancel one another, while masses only add. A planet accumulates a huge mass, and its charges are compensated."

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.p4"
text = "Another difference: the acceleration in a gravitational field is the same for every body (mass appears in the force and in the inertia, and cancels), while in an electric field it depends on the ratio \\(\\ \\frac{{q}}{{m}}\\). Every body falls the same way. Electrons and protons in the same electric field speed up very differently."

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.p5"
text = "The simulation has no gravity. “Orbit” is the electric analogue of a planetary orbit: the same attractive force, \\(\\ \\sim \\frac{{1}}{{r^2}}\\). “Rutherford” has the same distance dependence, but the force repels, so the hyperbolas bend away from the nucleus rather than toward it."

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.predict"
text = "In the “Orbit” scene, raise \\(\\ k\\) so the orbit becomes an ellipse. Where does the charge move fastest?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.q1"
text = "Why does gravity rule in space when it is so weak?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.q2"
text = "Can gravity be shielded the way an electric field can?"

[[string]]
id = "help.gravity.q3"
text = "Does a charge on a Coulomb orbit follow Kepler’s laws?"

