[[string]]
id = "help.potential.a1"
text = "If the zero is at infinity, \\(\\ \\varphi\\) at a point is the work the field does in carrying a charge of \\(\\ +1\\) from that point out to infinity. Equivalently, it is the work you must do against the field to bring \\(\\ +1\\) in from infinity to here. Potential says how “high”, in energy, the point sits for a unit positive charge."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.a2"
text = "The force of a point charge points along the radius and depends only on the distance. On each small step the work depends only on how much the distance to the charge changed, not on a sideways part of the step. The total is only the difference of the distances at the start and at the end. By superposition the same rule extends to any system of charges at rest. It is no longer true for friction, or for the circulating electric field that a changing magnetic field produces — and there is no potential there."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.a3"
text = "The field is the slope of the potential (topic 10): how fast \\(\\ \\varphi\\) changes with distance. The slope of \\(\\ \\frac{{1}}{{r}}\\) is \\(\\ \\frac{{1}}{{r^2}}\\), so the field falls one power faster."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.answer1"
text = "\\(\\varphi = 0\\) everywhere on the line that runs through the middle, perpendicular to the charges: the contributions of \\(\\ +1\\) and \\(\\ -1\\) match in size and oppose in sign. The field there is not zero — it points from the plus toward the minus. A zero of potential does not mean a zero of field. On the background (F1) this is the white vertical line between the red and blue regions."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.answer2"
text = "It is not zero. The potential there is positive — both contributions are positive and they add. A zero field only means the potential does not change if you shift a little, not that the potential itself is zero. Check it with the probe (F5): \\(\\ |E| \\approx 0\\), while \\(\\ \\varphi > 0\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 7"

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.legend1"
text = "the work the field does on a charge \\(\\ q\\) as it goes from A to B, by any path."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.legend2"
text = "the potentials at points A and B."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.legend3"
text = "the potential: energy per unit charge, in volts."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.legend4"
text = "the potential energy of a test charge \\(\\ q\\) at this point (topic 8)."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.legend5"
text = "the source charge, with its sign. A negative \\(\\ Q\\) gives a negative potential."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.legend6"
text = "the sum of the contributions of every charge — ordinary numbers, with no directions."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.p1"
text = "Start from the fact without which there would be no potential. When a charge goes from point A to point B, the electrostatic field does work on it, and that work does not depend on the path — only on where the path started and where it ended. Fields like that are called conservative. Gravity is one of them too."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.p2"
text = "Because the work depends only on the endpoints, each point can be given a number so that the work equals the difference of those numbers. For a test charge \\(\\ q\\), that number is its potential energy \\(\\ U\\). \\(\\ U\\) is proportional to \\(\\ q\\), so dividing by \\(\\ q\\) leaves a quantity that describes only the point and the sources: the potential \\(\\ \\varphi\\), energy per unit charge. The unit is the volt: \\(\\ 1\\,\\mathrm{{V}} = 1\\,\\mathrm{{J}}/\\mathrm{{C}}\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.p3"
text = "Potential is a scalar, just a number at each point, so potentials from several charges add as ordinary numbers, with no directions. For a point charge, \\(\\ \\varphi = k\\frac{{Q}}{{r}}\\): the sign matches \\(\\ Q\\), and it falls more slowly than the field — inversely with distance. You choose the zero of potential yourself, usually at infinity. Only differences of potential have physical meaning (topics 9 and 12)."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.p4"
text = "An equipotential is a line (in space, a surface) along which \\(\\ \\varphi\\) is the same. The field is always perpendicular to the equipotentials, and moving along them takes no work — like walking along a contour line on a height map."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.p5"
text = "F1 is the potential background. Color shows the sign of \\(\\ \\varphi\\) (red positive, blue negative), brightness shows the magnitude, and the thin light lines are equipotentials every \\(\\ 0.25\\). The probe (F5) shows the exact value of \\(\\ \\varphi\\) under the cursor."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.predict1"
text = "In a dipole (\\(+1\\) and \\(\\ -1\\)), where is the potential zero? Is the field zero there too?"

[[string]]
id = "help.potential.predict2"
text = "Halfway between two equal charges of \\(\\ +1\\) the field is zero (topic 4). What about the potential?"

