text = "Try it: a dipole with the potential background and a test charge"

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.a1"
text = "Potential characterizes a point of the field, and it is the same for any charge placed there. Potential energy is the energy of one particular charge at one particular point. More precisely, \\(\\ U\\) is the energy of the interaction of the charge \\(\\ q\\) with the sources of the field — the energy of the whole configuration, not a “store” inside the charge (topic 14)."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.a2"
text = "Because \\(\\ \\varphi\\) was defined precisely so as to remove the dependence on the charge. The energy is proportional to \\(\\ q\\), and dividing by \\(\\ q\\) leaves only the contribution of the sources and of the position of the point. Going back from \\(\\ \\varphi\\) to \\(\\ U\\), you multiply by \\(\\ q\\) again — and the dependence on the charge returns."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.a3"
text = "Toward higher. Any charge seeks a lower energy \\(\\ U = q\\varphi\\). For \\(\\ q < 0\\) the energy is smaller when \\(\\ \\varphi\\) is larger, so electrons in a conductor flow toward points of higher potential — against the direction of the field \\(\\ E\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.a4"
text = "The energy a charge of one electron gains by crossing a potential difference of \\(\\ 1\\,\\mathrm{{V}}\\): \\(\\ 1\\,\\mathrm{{eV}} \\approx 1.6\\cdot 10^{{-19}}\\,\\mathrm{{J}}\\) (see the formula in topic 9). It is a convenient unit for atoms and particles: binding energies in atoms are a few electronvolts."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.answer1"
text = "\\(U = q\\varphi\\): for \\(\\ +2\\) it is 6, for \\(\\ -2\\) it is \\(\\ -6\\). The field at the point is one field. The energies differ, even in sign."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.answer2"
text = "\\(+1\\) heads toward lower \\(\\ \\varphi\\) — away from the positive charges, where the background is dimmer. \\(\\ -1\\) heads toward higher \\(\\ \\varphi\\) — toward the charges, into the brighter red region."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 8"

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.legend1"
text = "the potential of the point — set by the sources, independent of \\(\\ q\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.legend2"
text = "the charge whose energy we want, with its sign."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.legend3"
text = "the potential energy of that charge at that point: it depends on both \\(\\ \\varphi\\) and \\(\\ q\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.note"
text = "An example: at a point with potential 1000 V, a charge of \\(\\ +1\\) C has energy 1000 J, a charge of \\(\\ +2\\) C has 2000 J, and a charge of \\(\\ -1\\) C has minus 1000 J. The potential stays the same the whole time. Only the energy changes."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.p1"
text = "The potential \\(\\ \\varphi\\) and the potential energy \\(\\ U\\) are easy to mix up, and they are different quantities. Potential is a property of a point in space: the sources set it, and it is the same no matter which charge you put there. Potential energy is a property of one particular charge at that point: \\(\\ U = q\\varphi\\). Twice the charge means twice the energy, and a charge of the opposite sign has energy of the opposite sign."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.p2"
text = "It is the same pair of ideas as field and force. \\(\\ E = \\frac{{F}}{{q}}\\) describes the point; \\(\\ F = qE\\) describes one charge. Likewise \\(\\ \\varphi = \\frac{{U}}{{q}}\\) describes the point, and \\(\\ U = q\\varphi\\) describes one charge."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.p3"
text = "An important consequence follows. A charge in a field moves toward where its energy \\(\\ U\\) is smaller. For a positive charge that is a region of lower potential. For a negative charge it is the opposite, a region of higher potential: when \\(\\ q < 0\\), a smaller \\(\\ U\\) corresponds to a larger \\(\\ \\varphi\\). Pluses “roll down” the potential hill. Minuses “climb” it."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.p4"
text = "The probe (F5) is a charge of \\(\\ +1\\), so the \\(\\ \\varphi\\) it shows is both the potential of the point and the energy of that test charge there. The “Potential \\(\\ U\\)” row on the panel is the energy of every pair of charges in the scene together (topic 11)."

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.predict1"
text = "At a point the potential is \\(\\ \\varphi = 3\\). What energy do charges of \\(\\ +2\\) and \\(\\ -2\\) have there?"

[[string]]
id = "help.potential_energy.predict2"
text = "In the field of two equal positive charges (the “Like charges” scene), place a free \\(\\ +1\\) and a free \\(\\ -1\\) off to the side. Where does each one head, in terms of potential?"

