text = "How to use"

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.a1"
text = "It is the same unit. A volt is a joule per coulomb, and a joule is a newton times a meter. So \\(\\ \\mathrm{{V}}/\\mathrm{{m}} = \\mathrm{{J}}/(\\mathrm{{C}}\\cdot\\mathrm{{m}}) = \\mathrm{{N}}/\\mathrm{{C}}\\). The first form is handy when you are thinking about force; the second, when you are thinking about voltage and distance."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.a2"
text = "Not necessarily. The spark of static electricity from a doorknob is thousands of volts, but the charge is so small (millionths of a coulomb) that the energy of the discharge is only thousandths of a joule. What makes it dangerous is the energy and the current the source can deliver, not the voltage alone. It is a good illustration of the difference between potential and energy."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.a3"
text = "Charge flows (electrons). Potential only says how much energy the field hands to each coulomb between two points. A drop in potential along a wire or a lamp is a place where the field gives energy to the charges, and the charges give it to the conductor (heat, light)."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.answer1"
text = "In the middle, \\(\\ \\varphi\\) falls almost uniformly — from positive values near the pluses to negative values near the minuses, passing through zero halfway. \\(\\ |E|\\) hardly changes there: the field is uniform. Right next to the rows the picture is uneven, because the plates in the scene are built from separate charges."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.answer2"
text = "An amount equal to the potential difference between the rows: \\(\\ W = q\\Delta\\varphi\\) with \\(\\ q = 1\\). You can measure it with the probe — the difference of the \\(\\ \\varphi\\) readings near the pluses and near the minuses."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 9"

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.legend1"
text = "the voltage between points A and B, in volts."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.legend2"
text = "the work the field does on a charge \\(\\ q\\) going from A to B."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.legend3"
text = "the distance between equipotentials (for example, between the plates) along the field."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.legend4"
text = "the strength of a uniform field, \\(\\ \\mathrm{{V}}/\\mathrm{{m}} = \\mathrm{{N}}/\\mathrm{{C}}\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.legend5"
text = "the electronvolt — the energy a charge \\(\\ e\\) gains by crossing a voltage of \\(\\ 1\\,\\mathrm{{V}}\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.p1"
text = "The voltage between two points is the potential difference between them. That is the quantity with physical meaning: the work the field does on a charge going from one point to the other depends on it. A voltmeter always has two probes — it measures a difference, not a potential."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.p2"
text = "A warning about notation. School textbooks often use the letter \\(\\ U\\) for voltage. In this reference \\(\\ U\\) is potential energy, so voltage is written \\(\\ \\Delta\\varphi\\), to keep the two apart."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.p3"
text = "“1.5 V” on a battery means: between its terminals the potential difference is 1.5 V, so every coulomb of charge that passes around the outside circuit receives 1.5 J from the field. The chemistry of the battery maintains that difference."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.p4"
text = "The simplest example is the uniform field between the plates of a capacitor (topic 6). The field there is the same at every point, so the potential changes steadily, linearly with distance: the equipotentials are parallel, equally spaced lines. That is the link between field and voltage — and the second unit of field, \\(\\ \\mathrm{{V}}/\\mathrm{{m}}\\), which equals \\(\\ \\mathrm{{N}}/\\mathrm{{C}}\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.p5"
text = "The “Capacitor” scene: the background (F1) shows the potential falling linearly between the rows, the equipotentials between them are nearly parallel lines, and the field lines (F2) are parallel segments from the pluses to the minuses. The probe (F5) gives \\(\\ \\varphi\\) and \\(\\ |E|\\) at any point."

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.predict1"
text = "In the “Capacitor” scene, run the probe (F5) in a straight line from the upper row to the lower one. How does \\(\\ \\varphi\\) change? And \\(\\ |E|\\)?"

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.predict2"
text = "What kinetic energy does a charge of \\(\\ +1\\) gain if it flies from the upper row to the lower one?"

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.q1"
text = "Why is the field measured both in \\(\\ \\mathrm{{N}}/\\mathrm{{C}}\\) and in \\(\\ \\mathrm{{V}}/\\mathrm{{m}}\\)?"

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.q2"
text = "Is a high voltage dangerous by itself?"

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.q3"
text = "What “flows” in a circuit: potential, or charge?"

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.title"
text = "Voltage and a uniform field"

[[string]]
id = "help.voltage.try"
text = "Try it: “Capacitor” with potential, lines, and a test charge"

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.a1"
text = "Potential energy is the field’s stored ability to do work. When the field does positive work (it speeds a charge up), it spends that store, so \\(\\ U\\) decreases. When an outside force works against the field (it pushes like charges together), the field’s \\(\\ W\\) is negative, and the store is refilled. A falling stone is the same story: gravity does positive work, and the potential energy of the height falls."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.a2"
text = "That the force points toward decreasing \\(\\ U\\): \\(\\ F = -\\frac{{dU}}{{dx}}\\). From rest, the system therefore starts downhill on \\(\\ U\\) and, if there is friction or an inelastic collision, it eventually settles at a minimum of \\(\\ U\\). With no losses it overshoots the minimum and oscillates around it — \\(\\ U\\) falls and then rises, while \\(\\ K + U\\) stays constant."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.a3"
text = "Through the work of the field. On each small step the field does work \\(\\ dW\\): \\(\\ K\\) grows by exactly that much, and \\(\\ U\\) falls by exactly that much. There is no separate mechanism of “flowing”. It is one process, written two ways."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.a4"
text = "Almost, with known exceptions. The integrator (the appendix) keeps \\(\\ K + U\\) to about a tenth of a percent: an automatic test checks that over 20,000 steps. Inelastic collisions (coefficient 0.8) remove kinetic energy, the speed cap clips it on very close flybys, and dragging with the mouse adds energy from outside."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.answer1"
text = "In opposite phase: when the charge is closer to the center, \\(\\ U\\) is more negative and \\(\\ K\\) is larger; when it is farther, the reverse. Their sum \\(\\ E\\) on the panel (at the new \\(\\ k\\)) hardly changes."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.answer2"
text = "It falls because of inelastic collisions: at each impact some kinetic energy disappears (in reality it would have become heat). With collisions off, the total energy is nearly constant. Opposite charges pass through one another and are fast at the moment they meet, then slow down again. They started from rest, so not every one flies off forever — only those that a few neighbors sling outward."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.kicker"
text = "TOPIC 13"

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.legend1"
text = "the work of the field on the path from A to B: the sum of force times small displacements."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.legend2"
text = "the dot product: only the component of the force along the displacement does work."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.legend3"
text = "the change in kinetic energy — the sum of the energies of motion of every free charge."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.legend4"
text = "the change in the potential energy of the system."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.p1"
text = "The work of a force is how much the force helps the motion: the force times the displacement along it, added up along the path. The total work of all the forces equals the change in the kinetic energy of the body (the work–energy theorem). For an electrostatic field, as for any conservative force, that same work can be written through the potential energy: it equals the drop in potential energy, \\(\\ \\Delta U = -W\\)."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.p2"
text = "Put the two facts together: what the field adds to the kinetic energy, it takes from the potential energy. The sum \\(\\ K + U\\) does not change — that is conservation of mechanical energy. Potential energy turns into kinetic energy and back, as in a pendulum."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.p3"
text = "The phrase “a system moves toward lower potential energy” needs a precise reading. The force always points downhill on \\(\\ U\\), so a system released from rest starts toward lower \\(\\ U\\). Inertia can then carry it back up the slope: a charge on an elliptical orbit moves closer to the center and then farther away, and its \\(\\ U\\) falls and then rises. Only the sum \\(\\ K + U\\) stays fixed."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.p4"
text = "Where does conservation of energy come from? The deep answer is Noether’s theorem: the laws of physics are the same today and tomorrow (symmetry under a shift in time), and from that symmetry it follows that the total energy of an isolated system is conserved."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.p5"
text = "The energy panel shows \\(\\ K\\) (the kinetic energy of the free charges), \\(\\ U\\) (the sum over pairs), and \\(\\ E = K + U\\). Trails (F6) show what that exchange of energy looks like in the motion."

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.predict1"
text = "In the “Orbit” scene, raise \\(\\ k\\) with the slider so the orbit becomes elliptical. How do \\(\\ K\\) and \\(\\ U\\) behave on the panel?"

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.predict2"
text = "In “Swarm”, watch the total energy \\(\\ E = K + U\\). Why does it fall over time? What changes if you turn collisions off (K)?"

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.q1"
text = "Why is \\(\\ \\Delta U = -W\\), with that minus sign?"

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.q2"
text = "What does “a system moves toward lower potential energy” actually mean?"

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.q3"
text = "How exactly does potential energy turn into kinetic energy?"

[[string]]
id = "help.work_motion.q4"
text = "Does the program conserve energy?"

